[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a minute if you need it, then tell me how you'd structure your approach.

[candidate silent 25s]

[candidate · 1:55] Okay. Since revenue is actually up 15% and margin collapsed from 24% to 6%, this reads as a cost problem rather than a demand problem — though I want to confirm revenue growth isn't coming from heavy discounting that inflates traffic while hurting price per cup.

I'd structure it as: first, decompose profit into revenue and costs. On revenue, split into number of transactions times average ticket, and check store count — 200 locations, but how many were opened in the last two years? New stores ramping could drag margin. On costs, split fixed versus variable: COGS (coffee beans, milk, cups), labor, rent and occupancy, and corporate overhead.

Then I'd isolate which cost line grew fastest as a percent of revenue and drill into that.

To start: do we have a cost breakdown as a percent of revenue for both years?
